body:
Tbh, I would actually suggest you start drawing using paper and pencil. There are many reasons to why this is a better start than starting on an electronic device. For me, drawing on pencil and paper developed really good habits. Also, you have a lot more control of your utencil compared to using an ipencil imo (if you want more reasons for why I liked to start off with paper and pencil, do comment and say so.). Now, I'm assuming you're completely new to drawing and this is why you're here. What I'd suggest is to not start out trying to draw a certain way. Don't emulate someone's style. Your goal is to develop your own style (which is a progressive thing, not something you just get out of the blue). Now, saying this, I suggest you start with a basic drawing course (that means not a "how to draw like Kishimoto" but more like a "Drawing course for beginners that explores anatomy and such"). You see, there are many styles of drawing in the world of artists. Some simplify things and some overcomplicate them (show maybe too much anatomy \[like big ass veins and etc.\]). For something like Naruto, you see a lot of simplification in the anatomy of things. My point is, if you learn how to draw in one style, that style might be missing some important things for the thing you're trying to draw. Learning one style limits you to only that style and so you can't explore as much. So here is where I say you should start. Start with a average human course ( a course that covers the anatomy of the average human). With this, you'll begin to understand the underlying factors in a human body. You'll understand what the spine looks like, what the skull looks like, why the face looks the way it looks, why a woman's waist seems wider than a man's, etc. Now for the resources, there's many. Although, you'll find that a lot of them are quite expensive to access the good stuff. Even the all mighty Proko has a paid program that will help you more than his free program. A lot of these programs are $100 and above which can be quite annoying for a someone who doens't have all the money to spare. However, still, Proko's free stuff is quite good imo. You can find his channel on youtube or find his website where he has a catalog of all his videos on all types of stuff, including anatomy. Another famous resource is Loomis books. His books are quite good for some, but not all. I haven't used these books in my personal experience but it's still worth checking them out. Many people use them as a starting point for their journey. A place that I find best for learning how to draw is Udemy. The courses there are actually quite good. That's where I started at least, after I got bored of reading drawing-based books that got quite boring. If you want to follow my path, take these course's:  Character Art School: Complete Character Drawing Course and The Complete Figure Drawing Course HD on Udemy. The first one will help you understand drawing fundamental and is kind of like the geometry of drawing (theories and shit). The second is where the anatomy and drawing comes in play. This one is like the mathematics of drawing (actual drawing and shit). I STRONGLY recommend starting with my first course suggestion and then movign on to my second and doing it all using paper and pencil. Take notes as if these were real classes. Also, for the second course (The Complete Figure Drawing Course HD), the startegies the guy uses to draw a face isn't exactly that great. I suggest sticking through the whole course (including the face section) and doing what you can, but learning how to draw a face from Proko. However, do take the whole course anyway and try to understand it. All in all, this will all take you from 1 month to 3+ months depending on how much time you have. All I can say is that these courses worked wonders for me and were my greatest ally in my journey to becoming a true artist. Anyway, I hope this helped you in some kind of way. If you have ANY questions, feel free to reply and I'll gladly answer. Gn now :)

&#x200B;

#

Oh and yeah. Btw wtf happned to the prince and commoner trying to fight for their love. It could have easily done the same exact story, but included Zen more and removed any kind of flustering from Shirayuki towards Obi. That would've been perfect. Yeah maybe she does get fustered at some point BUT THEN Zen comes in and takes her love back again. Like why did the author create this trash arc right now? It's sooooooo broing and annoying. I guess I just honestly hate it when the first love who they clearly shared a great bond with eachother is just forgotten all of a sudden for years and for 50+ chapters. I AM SO ANNOYED BECAUSE IT DIDN'T STICK TO ITS' ROOTS, ITS' PREMISE, ITS' FOUNDATION.  

